1. High Concept

Fourfold Accord is a poker-based roguelike where the player builds a run around four suit factions, each with its own identity and progression path. Instead of suits being only part of hand evaluation, suits act as strategic factions that grow stronger as they are used throughout a run.

The game keeps the satisfying loop of:

drawing cards,
forming poker hands,
scoring against a target,
collecting passive modifiers and consumables,

but adds two core twists:

Suit Identity
Hearts, Spades, Diamonds, and Clubs each have a persistent gameplay role within a run.
Suit Mastery
Repeated use of a suit increases that suit’s mastery level, upgrading its passive effect and gradually shaping the player’s build.

The goal is to create a game that feels familiar at the level of poker-hand deckbuilding, but distinct in its internal logic, progression, and thematic presentation.

2. Design Goals
Primary Goals
Build a playable prototype with a full run loop
Preserve the satisfying “score-chasing card combo” feel
Differentiate the project through suit-based build identity
Keep the implementation scope manageable for a solo student project
Secondary Goals
Make build direction readable and intentional
Encourage strategic adaptation, not blind copying of one dominant pattern
Support later expansion with more modifiers, consumables, and boss mechanics
3. Core Design Pillars
3.1 Familiar Poker Core

The player forms traditional poker hands from a changing hand of cards:

High Card
Pair
Two Pair
Three of a Kind
Straight
Flush
Full House
Four of a Kind
Straight Flush
3.2 Suit-Based Build Identity

Each suit is more than a visual property. Each suit represents a different strategic role:

Hearts = stability / chips
Spades = burst / multiplier
Diamonds = economy / resources
Clubs = technique / retrigger / utility
3.3 Growth Through Play

Build direction is shaped by what the player repeatedly plays during the run. The system should feel like:

“this run is becoming a Hearts run”
“my Clubs route is online”
“my Spades engine is stronger now”

rather than only:

“I found one lucky passive card”
3.4 Controlled Scope

The prototype should focus on:

the main loop,
scoring,
suit mastery,
a small set of passive modifiers,
a small set of consumables,
one boss rule layer.

Not full production-level content volume.

4. Thematic Framing

The game is framed as a conflict between four allied sigils/factions and a powerful boss presence. Suits are presented as faction-aligned forces rather than standard card suit flavor only.

Naming System
Game title: Fourfold Accord
Suit system: Sigils
Joker-like passive cards: Champions
Tarot-like consumables: Stratagems
Planet-like hand upgrades: Doctrines
Spectral-like high-risk cards: Forbidden Oaths
Vouchers / persistent shop upgrades: Edicts
Shop: Quartermaster
Boss: Overseer

This supports a more original presentation and world logic.

5. Core Gameplay Loop
Per Run
Start run
Enter an encounter structure
Beat score targets across multiple rounds
Gain gold and rewards
Visit Quartermaster
Buy Champions, consumables, or upgrades
Continue until defeat or run completion
Per Encounter
Draw to hand limit
Select cards to play
Form poker hand
Score hand
Apply suit effects
Apply Champion effects
Replace played/discarded cards
Continue until target is met or actions run out
6. Round Structure

A run is divided into cycles.
Each cycle contains:

Skirmish
Battle
Overseer Fight

This structure mirrors escalating pressure:

first round: easier scoring
second round: stronger requirement
third round: boss rule / major restriction

The prototype does not need a huge number of cycles.
A good prototype target is 3 cycles total.

7. Basic Match Rules
Starting Hand Size

8 cards

Actions Per Encounter
Hands: 4
Discards: 3
Hand Play Rules
Player may play 1 to 5 cards
The selected set is evaluated as the scoring hand
Played cards are removed
New cards are drawn to restore hand size
Discard Rules
Player may discard selected cards
Draw the same number of replacement cards
Hand content changes dynamically throughout the encounter
Win / Loss
Reach the target score before running out of Hands → win
Run out of Hands before reaching target → lose
8. Core Scoring Structure

The prototype scoring should be intentionally simpler than a full commercial game.

Step 1: Determine Poker Hand Type

Evaluate the played cards and identify the best valid hand type.

Step 2: Apply Base Hand Score

Each hand type has:

base chips
base multiplier

Example prototype table:

Hand Type	Base Chips	Base Mult
High Card	10	1.0
Pair	20	1.2
Two Pair	40	1.5
Three of a Kind	60	2.0
Straight	100	2.0
Flush	120	2.5
Full House	160	3.0
Four of a Kind	220	4.0
Straight Flush	300	5.0
Step 3: Add Played Card Value

Optional prototype rule:

scoring cards add small chip bonuses based on rank
Step 4: Apply Suit Effects

Suit identity effects modify the current scoring context.

Step 5: Apply Champion Effects

Champions resolve from left to right.

Step 6: Final Score

Final score = Chips × Mult

9. Suit Identity System

This is the main differentiating mechanic.

Key Principle

Suit effects should not heavily reward stacking one suit by raw quantity, or the game may overly encourage Flush-only play.

Instead, suit effects should reward:

suit presence,
role-based contribution,
controlled synergy.
Suit Roles
Hearts — Stability

Role: reliable chip generation

Prototype effect:

If at least 1 scoring Heart is present, gain a flat chip bonus

Example:

Heart effect: +10 chips
Upgrades improve the flat value

Design intent:

Hearts support many hand types
Not dependent on playing all-Hearts hands
Spades — Burst

Role: multiplier spike

Prototype effect:

The highest-rank scoring Spade grants extra multiplier

Example:

Highest scoring Spade grants +0.5 mult

Design intent:

Spades act like a “carry” suit
Encourages precision, not stacking all Spades
Diamonds — Economy

Role: gold and shop value

Prototype effect:

If 1–2 scoring Diamonds are present, gain +1 gold after scoring

Design intent:

Diamonds encourage mixed hands
Prevents “more Diamonds always better”
Clubs — Technique

Role: utility / retrigger

Prototype effect:

If at least 1 scoring Club is present, retrigger one scoring card’s base value once

Design intent:

Clubs reward setup and finesse
Best as utility support, not necessarily full-suit focus
10. Suit Mastery System
Purpose

Repeated use of a suit increases its power across the run.

This is the prototype’s main progression twist.

Rule

A suit gains Mastery XP if it participates in a scoring hand.

Important balancing rule:

A suit gains XP once per hand played, not once per card of that suit
This prevents the system from overly rewarding Flush spam

Example:

If a played hand includes Hearts, Hearts gain +1 XP
Even if the hand contains 4 Hearts, Hearts still only gain +1 XP that hand
Mastery Levels

Prototype target:

Level 0
Level 1
Level 2
Level 3

Example thresholds:

Level 1: 3 XP
Level 2: 6 XP
Level 3: 10 XP
Example Level Progression
Hearts
Lv0: +10 chips if a Heart is present
Lv1: +15 chips
Lv2: +20 chips
Lv3: +20 chips and +0.5 mult if 2+ Hearts are present
Spades
Lv0: highest scoring Spade gives +0.5 mult
Lv1: +0.8 mult
Lv2: +1.0 mult
Lv3: +1.0 mult and +10 chips
Diamonds
Lv0: 1–2 Diamonds → +1 gold
Lv1: +2 gold
Lv2: +2 gold and shop refresh discount
Lv3: +3 gold
Clubs
Lv0: retrigger 1 scoring card’s base value
Lv1: retrigger stronger target selection
Lv2: retrigger + small flat chips
Lv3: retrigger plus +0.5 mult
11. Champions (Passive Modifier Cards)

Champions are the prototype’s equivalent of Joker-like passive build cards.

Slot Limit

5 slots

Resolution Rule

Champions resolve from left to right

This keeps the system understandable and visually readable.

Champion Design Goals
Small number in prototype: 6 to 10
Focus on synergy with Suit Mastery and core poker hands
Avoid huge complexity in first prototype
Example Champions
Crimson Banner

Hearts mastery effects are 50% stronger

Black Omen

If Spades are at Level 2+, gain an additional +0.5 mult when a Spade contributes

Gilded Ledger

Diamonds require 1 less XP to level up

Thorn Echo

Clubs retrigger one additional base-value effect, once per hand

Oathbound Twin

If exactly 2 suits are represented in the played hand, gain +20 chips

Veteran Marshal

First hand played in each encounter gains +30 chips

Accord Keeper

If all four suits have gained at least 1 XP this cycle, gain +1 permanent hand action next encounter

12. Consumable Card Types

The prototype does not need all categories fully implemented.
A small set is enough.

12.1 Stratagems

One-time tactical cards

Examples:

Change a selected card’s suit
Copy a selected card
Destroy a selected card
Upgrade one suit’s mastery by +1 XP
Temporarily increase hand size next encounter
12.2 Doctrines

Upgrade a poker-hand archetype

Examples:

Pair gains +10 chips
Straight gains +0.5 mult
Full House gains +20 chips

These act as longer-term scoring upgrades.

12.3 Forbidden Oaths

High-risk, high-reward effects

Examples:

Gain a powerful Champion but lose max discard
Destroy 2 random cards, gain massive gold
Set one suit to Lv3 for the next encounter, but reduce another suit to Lv0

Prototype can include only 1–2 of these if time allows.

13. Card Growth / “Card History” System

This should be kept small in prototype scope.

Important Design Clarification

This is not account-level permanent progression.
It is run-level card history.

Each playing card is an instance with its own state.

Why This Matters

It distinguishes the system from generic passive modifiers:

cards in deck have identity
cards can develop value through usage
player may care about preserving or modifying a specific card
Playing Card Fields

Each card instance stores:

suit
rank
unique ID
times played
times scored
times discarded
trait status (optional)
mastery-linked history flag
Prototype Version Recommendation

Keep this very small:

cards do not level multiple times
a card may gain one historical trait at most
Example Trait Triggers
Veteran

Trigger:

card has been part of scoring hands 3 times

Effect:

+8 chips when scored
Flow

Trigger:

card contributed to the same suit-aligned scoring line repeatedly

Effect:

gains a small bonus if its suit mastery is Level 2+
Stubborn

Trigger:

card has been discarded 3 times but not destroyed

Effect:

first time it scores each encounter, +10 chips
Scope Warning

If time becomes tight, this system can be reduced or cut without breaking the prototype, because Suit Mastery already provides strong identity.

14. Quartermaster (Shop) System
Purpose

Let the player shape the run after each encounter.

Prototype Shop Inventory

Show 3 choices:

Champion
Stratagem
Doctrine
Shop Actions
Buy
Reroll
